  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nQuiet and dry weather dominates the Northland for the rest of \ntoday and tonight. Surface high pressure centered to our east \nkeeps skies mostly sunny to clear across the region, aside from \nsome mid to high clouds in NW WI associated with an upper-level\nlow spinning southward through the northern Plains. Continuing \nthe trend from the last forecast package, dewpoints were lowered\nto better match observations across the Northland. As a result,\nafternoon relative humidity will dip into the 30s, with \nisolated spots in the upper 20s. Winds will remain light tonight,\nallowing another cold night under mostly clear skies.\n\nDry and stagnant conditions persist through Thanksgiving Day and\nThursday night. The upper-level low over our region will merge with\na broader series of troughs in the southeastern states, leaving\nthe Northland quietly between systems. Afternoon relative \nhumidity will again dip into the 30s, with isolated spots in the\nupper 20s, due to the lowered dewpoints. With light winds \ncontinuing, holiday travel across northeast Minnesota and \nnorthwest Wisconsin will remain optimal.\n\nDry weather holds through much of Friday, with afternoon relative\nhumidity dipping into the 30s and isolated upper 20s under winds\n10-15 mph. Changes arrive Friday evening and Friday night as a \ncold front from a low pressure system in Ontario moves through, \nleading to low chances for precipitation. However, low moisture \nstands the question of whether precipitation will even occur \nacross the area. If precipitation does occur, models are hinting\nat the possibility of freezing drizzle among mixed wintry\nprecipitation depending on the timing of the precipitation and \nsurface temperatures. Uncertainty is still very high, and \ncurrent temperature trends suggest that freezing drizzle, if any\ndevelops, could happen later Friday night. Again, however, due\nto low QPF expected, any accumulation will be little to none. \n\nHeading into Saturday and Sunday, moisture from a West Coast\natmospheric river feeds into the low pressure system tracking just\nnorth of the Canadian border. The Northland will likely only catch\nthe southern edge of this system, keeping much of the region dry or\nlimiting amounts to a few hundredths of an inch of liquid equivalent\nor a light dusting of snow. Precipitation type will depend largely\non the time of day, favoring snow at night and rain or a wintry mix\nduring the day. Even if the low takes a more southerly track and\nslightly boosts totals along the International Border, travel\nimpacts should remain minimal. Behind the system, blustery west to\nnorthwest winds are expected Saturday into Sunday, bringing breezy\nconditions over land and potentially hazardous conditions on western\nLake Superior.\n\nEarly next week, the upper-level pattern shifts to a quasi-zonal\nflow across the Upper Midwest, allowing weak upper-level troughs to\npass through the Northland on Monday and Tuesday. This setup will\nbring hit-or-miss chances for light rain or snow, particularly by\nMonday night and Tuesday. Overall impacts look minimal, with no\nlarge storms signaled in model guidance and temperatures across the\nregion hovering near to slightly above seasonal averages.",
